.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm xtc_cfg
is a typed runtime configuration registry, modeled on PostgreSQL's
GUC system.  Modules register named knobs at startup; operators
set them by name; values are validated against type and bounds.
.Pp
Supported types:
.Dv bool , int , int64 , double , string , enum
.Pq Vt xtc_cfg_kind_t .
.Pp
There is ONE registration entry point,
.Fn xtc_cfg_register ,
taking a
.Vt xtc_cfg_spec_t :
.Bl -bullet -compact -offset indent
.It
.Va name ,
the canonical name
.Pq dot-separated, e.g. Sq rexis.max_clients .
.It
.Va short_desc ,
an ops-friendly description.
.It
.Va kind ,
one of the
.Dv XTC_CFG_*
kinds.
.It
.Va dflt ,
a union holding the default for that kind.
.It
.Va min_int / Va max_int
and
.Va min_double / Va max_double ,
inclusive bounds for the numeric kinds
.Pq Li 0 / Li 0 means unbounded .
.It
.Va enum_labels / Va n_enum_labels
for
.Dv XTC_CFG_ENUM :
the allowed labels, the int value being the index into them.
.It
.Va validator , Va on_change ,
and
.Va cb_user ,
optional callbacks.
.El
.Pp
.Sy Storage is internal to the registry .
The spec carries NO backing-storage pointer: a registered variable
lives inside the registry and is read back through the accessors below,
not through a caller-supplied variable that the registry writes.
A
value set with
.Fn xtc_cfg_set_int
is observed with
.Fn xtc_cfg_get_int
or, on a hot path, through an
.Vt xtc_cfg_ref_t .
.Fn xtc_cfg_unregister
removes a knob by name.
.Pp
There is no string-parsing single setter: the typed setters
.Fn xtc_cfg_set_bool ,
.Fn xtc_cfg_set_int ,
.Fn xtc_cfg_set_int64 ,
.Fn xtc_cfg_set_double ,
.Fn xtc_cfg_set_string ,
and
.Fn xtc_cfg_set_enum
assign a value of the matching C type, validating it against the
registered bounds and validator and firing the on-change callback.
String parsing happens only in
.Fn xtc_cfg_load_file ,
which reads
.Sq name = value
lines;
.Fn xtc_cfg_reload
re-reads the last loaded file.
.Pp
Read a value with
.Fn xtc_cfg_get_bool ,
.Fn xtc_cfg_get_int ,
.Fn xtc_cfg_get_int64 ,
.Fn xtc_cfg_get_double ,
.Fn xtc_cfg_get_string ,
or
.Fn xtc_cfg_get_enum
for the corresponding registered kind.
.Pp
.Fn xtc_cfg_count
returns the number of registered knobs;
.Fn xtc_cfg_kind
reports the
.Vt xtc_cfg_kind_t
of a named knob.
.Pp
For a knob read on a hot path, the name-keyed getters are too slow:
each does a registry lookup.
.Fn xtc_cfg_ref
resolves a name to an opaque, pointer-stable
.Vt xtc_cfg_ref_t
handle once; thereafter
.Fn xtc_cfg_ref_get_bool ,
.Fn xtc_cfg_ref_get_int ,
.Fn xtc_cfg_ref_get_int64 ,
.Fn xtc_cfg_ref_get_double ,
.Fn xtc_cfg_ref_get_string ,
and
.Fn xtc_cfg_ref_get_enum
read the current value with no name lookup, still observing live
.Fn xtc_cfg_set_*
updates.
The handle stays valid until the variable is unregistered, so it can
be cached for the process lifetime like a compiled-in pointer.
.Ss Per-session scoping
A single global value per name is what a database server cannot use for
most settings.  A session layers per-variable OVERRIDES over the global
registry: a read resolves the session override first, then the global
value, and a session set never touches the global value.
.Fn xtc_cfg_session_create
and
.Fn xtc_cfg_session_destroy
manage a session;
.Fn xtc_cfg_session_bind
makes one the current session for the calling fiber (returning the
previous binding) so the unscoped getters resolve through it, and
.Fn xtc_cfg_session_current
reports the bound session.  The binding is per-FIBER, not per-thread: it
is keyed on the calling fiber's identity, so two fibers on one loop can
each bind their own session, and a work-stolen fiber that resumes on a
different thread keeps its binding.
Off a fiber
.Pq a plain OS thread
it is per-thread.
Within a session the overrides form a
transactional stack of levels:
.Fn xtc_cfg_session_push
opens a level,
.Fn xtc_cfg_session_commit
folds it into its parent (the values survive), and
.Fn xtc_cfg_session_abort
discards it -- backing SET LOCAL, SET inside a rolled-back transaction,
function-local SET, and subtransactions.
.Fn xtc_cfg_ssn_set_bool ,
.Fn xtc_cfg_ssn_set_int ,
.Fn xtc_cfg_ssn_set_int64 ,
.Fn xtc_cfg_ssn_set_double ,
.Fn xtc_cfg_ssn_set_string ,
and
.Fn xtc_cfg_ssn_set_enum
set a per-session override at the current level, each carrying a source
rank
.Po Dv XTC_CFG_SRC_DEFAULT
through
.Dv XTC_CFG_SRC_OVERRIDE Pc ;
a lower-ranked source cannot replace a higher one, and
.Fn xtc_cfg_session_source
reports the winner.
.Fn xtc_cfg_session_reset
drops the current level's override for a name (RESET), so the parent
level or the global value shows through again.
.Pp
Unregistering a variable that a live session still overrides is safe in
EITHER order: the entry's storage is refcounted and outlives the
unregister until the last override naming it is torn down, while being
immediately invisible to every lookup.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
Every function returning
.Vt int
returns
.Dv XTC_OK
on success and a negative
.Dv XTC_E_*
code on failure:
.Dv XTC_E_INVAL
for a NULL argument, a kind mismatch, a validator rejection, a
duplicate registration, or an unregistered name passed to a name-keyed
getter, setter,
.Fn xtc_cfg_kind
or
.Fn xtc_cfg_unregister ;
.Dv XTC_E_RANGE
for a value outside the registered bounds
.Pq or outside Li 0 Ns .. Ns Li 1 for a bool, or not a label index for an enum ,
from the global and the per-session setters alike;
.Dv XTC_E_NOTFOUND
for an unregistered name passed to
.Fn xtc_cfg_ref ,
.Fn xtc_cfg_session_source
or
.Fn xtc_cfg_session_reset ;
.Dv XTC_E_NOMEM
on allocation failure.
There is no distinct code for a duplicate registration; an earlier
version of this page named one that does not exist in
.In xtc.h .
.Fn xtc_cfg_session_source
reports
.Dv XTC_CFG_SRC_DEFAULT
for a name the session does not override.
.Fn xtc_cfg_count
returns the count.
.Fn xtc_cfg_session_bind
and
.Fn xtc_cfg_session_current
return a session pointer or
.Dv NULL .
.Fn xtc_cfg_session_destroy
returns nothing and is NULL-safe.
.Pp
Releases before 1.50 returned
.Dv XTC_E_INVAL
from the per-session setters
.Pq Fn xtc_cfg_ssn_set_*
for an out-of-bounds value, while the global setters already returned
.Dv XTC_E_RANGE ;
both now return
.Dv XTC_E_RANGE .
.Pp
A value from a configuration file is parsed WIDE and then range-checked
against the target C type, so
.Sq 4294967297
for an
.Dv XTC_CFG_INT
knob is rejected as invalid rather than silently truncated to 1.
.Sh EXAMPLES
.Bd -literal -offset indent
static const xtc_cfg_spec_t spec = {
	.name       = "rexis.max_clients",
	.short_desc = "Maximum concurrent client connections",
	.kind       = XTC_CFG_INT,
	.dflt       = { .d_int = 1000 },
	.min_int    = 1,
	.max_int    = 100000,
};
int v;

if (xtc_cfg_register(&spec) != XTC_OK)
	return -1;

/* Operator runtime change, then read it back. */
(void)xtc_cfg_set_int("rexis.max_clients", 5000);
(void)xtc_cfg_get_int("rexis.max_clients", &v);     /* v == 5000 */

/* Hot path: resolve once, read with no name lookup. */
xtc_cfg_ref_t ref;
if (xtc_cfg_ref("rexis.max_clients", &ref) == XTC_OK)
	(void)xtc_cfg_ref_get_int(ref, &v);
.Ed
